Related exchange, broken, and ring ideographs also have negative responses.
Merged words usually weaken the response; repayment and restoration retain it.
Preceding punctuation strengthens standalone return characters.  Byte
fragments are joined so that split characters are recognized when completed.
Vietnamese còn/hoàn and split Korean hwan have additional negative responses;
the name/title Khan has a positive one.  Japanese 還元 (reduction / refund)
continues the response onto 元. At document starts, leading-spaced lowercase
ASCII words get a small positive baseline, and digits a small negative one.
Other tokens are zero. These are lexical rules, not an abstract meaning detector.
